f:Tbab,{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is Shaivism?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Shaivism is the tradition centered on Shiva as the Supreme Being — the cosmic yogi, destroyer, regenerator, and lord of consciousness. It is one of the four major denominations of Hinduism and spans multiple philosophical schools including Kashmir Shaivism and Shaiva Siddhanta."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is Vaishnavism?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Vaishnavism centers on Vishnu and his avatars (especially Krishna and Rama) as the Supreme. It is the largest Hindu denomination by global following and encompasses traditions from Sri Vaishnavism (South India) to the Gaudiya Vaishnavism of ISKCON."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Are Shaivites and Vaishnavas in conflict?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Historically, there were theological debates and occasionally communal tensions. Today, the two traditions coexist respectfully. Adi Shankaracharya's Shanmata system explicitly treats Shiva and Vishnu as equal manifestations of one Brahman."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which path is more philosophical?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Both have rigorous philosophical traditions. Kashmir Shaivism is among the most sophisticated philosophical systems ever developed. Vishishtadvaita and Dvaita — the philosophical backbones of Vaishnavism — are equally systematic."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the difference in their view of liberation?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In Shaivism: liberation is the recognition of one's own nature as Shiva-consciousness. In Vaishnavism: liberation (Mukti) is typically understood as eternal devotional relationship with Vishnu, with the soul retaining its identity."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can someone worship both Shiva and Vishnu?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Absolutely. The Harihara form — half Shiva, half Vishnu — is worshipped in temples across India. The Smarta tradition (following Adi Shankaracharya) treats all deities as manifestations of one Brahman and actively worships both."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which tradition is the Bhagavad Gita associated with?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The Gita features Krishna (a Vaishnava avatar) as the teacher. It is most central to Vaishnavism and is the key scripture for Gaudiya Vaishnavas, ISKCON, and Sri Vaishnavas. However, Adi Shankaracharya (Shaiva) also wrote the most acclaimed Advaitic commentary on it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I know which path is right for me?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Consider: Do you feel more drawn to meditative silence, asceticism, and dissolution of the ego (Shaiva tendency)? Or do you feel drawn to loving relationship with a personal God, devotional singing, and service (Vaishnava tendency)? The Faith Finder can help you explore this."}}]}7:["$","$1","c",{"children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"min-h-screen bg-background text-foreground selection:bg-orange-500/30 selection:text-orange-100 flex flex-col","children":[["$","$Le",null,{"slug":"shaivism-vs-vaishnavism","pillar":"spiritual-traditions"}],["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"$f"}}],"$L10","$L11","$L12","$L13"]}],["$L14","$L15"],"$L16"]}]
